<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19870083</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2132184</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>STUDIES ON THE QUANTITATIVE ACTION OF A SPECIFIC ENZYME IN TYPE III PNEUMOCOCCUS DERMAL INFECTION IN RABBITS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Goodner</surname><given-names>Kenneth</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dubos</surname><given-names>Ren&#x000e9;</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1dbb110N0x3272538">From the Hospital of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>30</day><month>9</month><year>1932</year></pub-date><volume>56</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>521</fpage><lpage>530</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>2</day><month>6</month><year>1932</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1932, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The enzyme which specifically decomposes the capsular polysaccharide of Type III Pneumococcus must be used in certain definite amounts in order to bring about the recovery of rabbits infected intradermally with this organism. The experiments reported in this paper indicate that the minimal amounts of enzyme required bear a definite relationship to the severity of the infection as gauged by the number of pneumococci present in the circulating blood. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>